the different databases that

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.540
are currently in use.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.780
When you look at this
chart, you can actually

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.820
make lot of different
inferences in the chart,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.457
depending on which eye you
use to look at the chart.

00:01:58.457 --> 00:02:00.290
But for me, the key
thing here is the number

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.810
of different databases
that are currently popular

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.360
and the number of different
types of databases

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.620
that are currently being
used to build applications.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:10.979
It's surprising.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.160
When you look at the
different kinds of use cases

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.860
that have evolved over
the last 10 years,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.560
it's very clear that using
a single relational database

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.760
was not going to cut it.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.816
And the reason being different
application use cases

00:02:22.816 --> 00:02:25.190
have different access patterns,
different query patterns.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.230
So using just
relational database,

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.990
even if the relational database
had different data models,

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.650
was not optimal.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.310
So over a period of time,
different kinds of databases

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.647
came into the market.

00:02:36.647 --> 00:02:38.480
And one of the key
trends that we are seeing

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.630
is this notion of
polyglot persistence.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:42.980
I just like the word polyglots.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.720
I'm like, OK, I
have to use this.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.660
But more importantly,
what we see

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.110
is the shift towards
more adoption

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.360
of microservices-based
architecture.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.010
And what that means
is when you use

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.660
microservices-based
architecture,

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.850
one of the key design
patterns is that you basically

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:01.790
have different databases,
purpose-built databases,

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.540
for whatever specific
microservices is using.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:06.330
So quick question.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:11.630
How many of you use just one
database in the applications

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:15.090
that you currently have?

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.790
What about, let's say three?

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.620
So the key point here is
that with microservices

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.440
architecture-- or not;
even if you are not--

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.500
the management of
databases start

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.780
becoming a lot more complex.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.530
When you use different
kinds of databases,

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.500
now you need to have new skill
sets that you need to have.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.380
Even if you can standardize
some of the automation,

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.150
or deployment, et
cetera, there are still

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.564
nuances across all these
different databases.

00:03:42.564 --> 00:03:44.980
What you want to be able to
do is just spend time building

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.670
applications, and less time
managing the infrastructure.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.000
So that's where, in the
context of this talk, where

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.640
managed services and managed
database services helps when

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.026
you move into a cloud platform.

00:03:58.026 --> 00:03:59.650
So the key thing
about managed services

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:02.290
is that it helps you focus
on the logical side of things

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.940
in terms of building
applications.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.520
And we take care of
the physical aspects

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.740
of managing the database.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.480
So in that context, Google
Cloud provides a wide variety

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.100
of managed database services.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.030
And since this is
the last session,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.529
I'm pretty sure you've attended
a lot of the database talks,

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:21.760
and you probably
have seen this slide.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.250
But the point here is that
regardless of what database

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.380
you are using, in
Google Cloud we

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.230
offer managed services
either provided by Google

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.850
or through our partner services.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:35.320
So if you look at just a quick
look at the different databases

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.327
that we offer on the
non-relational side,

00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:39.910
we have Cloud Data Store, which
is just kind of a document DB.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.160
Bigtable is a wide-column store.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.200
And with relational, we have
CloudSQL, which is essentially

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.130
PostgreS and MySQL;
and Spanner, which

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:52.030
is a highly scalable globally
distributed database.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.740
Today what I want to do is
focus on Cloud Memorystore.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:59.777
So what is Cloud Memorystore?

00:04:59.777 --> 00:05:01.860
So Cloud Memorystore, which
is currently in beta--

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.480
we released the
product back in May,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.030
and we are currently in beta--

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:10.720
is a fully managed in-memory
datastore service for Redis.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:16.240
So it's a fully managed
offering that supports Redis.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.780
So why is this important?

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.670
So if you ask anybody who
builds large-scale applications,

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.040
in memory caching there's
a fundamental piece

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.590
of that application
architecture.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.830
I don't think we need to spend
a lot of time kind of evaluating

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:31.780
the benefit of caching.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.942
But if you look at where
caching is applicable,

00:05:34.942 --> 00:05:36.400
there's a wide
variety of use cases

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.000
where caching is
truly beneficial,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.120
from a very simple web
application to anything

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.976
like Ad Tech, where
you're serving real time,

00:05:46.976 --> 00:05:49.420
or in the real-time
bidding infrastructure.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:53.680
So across the board, we
know that in Memorystore

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.130
that provides
extremely low latency,

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.630
query processing becomes
extremely beneficial.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.150
Or it's actually a
requirement these days

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.420
to have a cache in place.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.730
Now what are the different types
of caches that people normally

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:08.230
use?

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.810
There are two types of caches
that are very popular today.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.190
The most popular one is Redis.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.440
And then there's Memcache.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.640
Memcache has been
around for a long time.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:24.100
We actually have Brad here, who
worked on the initial versions

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.360
of Memcache.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.430
But Redis has become
a lot more popular.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.380
The reason Redis has
become a lot more popular

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.260
is because apart from
the performance aspects

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:35.050
that it gives, because
it's in Memorystore,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.670
it is mostly referred to
as a data structure server.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:42.220
So Memcache essentially allows
you to store key values,

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:47.170
and provides you quick
access to static data.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.840
But Redis provides
built-in data structure.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.670
So it actually expands the set
of use cases that can be used.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:56.890
And some other things,
like sorted sets,

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:59.740
are very popular for
building gaming applications.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.300
If you're building
leaderboards, sorted sets

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.660
becomes extremely useful.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:05.620
And there's lists.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.017
And there is [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:08.017 --> 00:07:09.850
So there's a wide variety
of data structures

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.370
that make it a very
powerful in Memorystore.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:14.880
But apart from that, I think
one of the issues with Memcache

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.450
was that if you really
relied on a cache,

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:19.450
and if you wanted
durability of keys,

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.580
Memcache was not really
the best solution for it.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.120
So with Redis you
have persistence.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.400
You also have replication.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.110
So if you really want to build
a highly resilient cache,

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.920
or in Memorystore,
Redis is actually

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.090
a good choice for that.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.220
Apart from that, it
also supports Pub/Sub.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.400
So if you want to
build notification

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:43.510
systems, et cetera, Redis
gives you that capability.

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:47.710
And also scripting.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.350
And all this that really,
really fast performance.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.697
So over a period of time, Redis
has become extremely popular.

00:07:54.697 --> 00:07:55.530
So a quick question.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:58.250
How many of you
actually use Memcache?

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:58.750
Wow.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:07:59.250
Quite a bit.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.540
What about Redis?

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.930
So it's interesting
for us, in terms

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:05.940
of when we look at
should we do Redis,

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.055
or should we do Memcache,
or should we provide both?

00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:11.430
But it's interesting to see
that it's an equal proportion

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:13.410
of both Redis and Memcache.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.500
And I think there's reasons
why Memcache sometimes

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.410
is much more useful for certain
use cases compared to Redis.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:27.850
So with Cloud Memorystore, we
provide Redis as the engine.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.350
The key thing that
we've been trying

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:30.850
to do with Cloud
Memorystore, like I said,

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:33.309
is that with managed services,
what we try to do is we

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:37.809
try to take the burden of
managing Redis yourself.

00:08:37.809 --> 00:08:40.539
And if you ask anybody
who has deployed Redis,

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:42.100
there's a lot of
things that goes

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.039
into managing
something on your own,

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.270
even as simple as things
like patching, for example.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:51.010
So with our managed services,
we take care of, essentially,

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.129
deploying Redis, and it
gives you an endpoint,

00:08:53.129 --> 00:08:55.420
and all you need to do is
basically worry about writing

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:57.169
applications against it.

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:58.960
The key thing I want
to say here is that we

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.510
are built on open source Redis.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:03.620
We are not building
anything Google proprietary.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.470
We are simply taking
the open source Redis

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.310
and exposing it as
a managed service.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:13.630
The other key things that
we wanted to focus on

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:17.140
was how do we provide a highly
reliable and highly available

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:18.160
service.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.900
So in the offering,
which I'll get

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:23.290
into in a little bit more
detail, we provide replication.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:26.260
We provide fast,
automatic failover.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.780
And we also provide an
available [? DSLA. ?]

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.300
So that's something that
normally you don't see.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.780
But we will be having
that once we go GA.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.480
With respect to replication
and automatic failover,

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.892
Sentinel is a common way
to set up replicated Redis.

00:09:42.892 --> 00:09:45.100
But there's a lot of complexity
in terms of deploying

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:46.880
Sentinel and managing it.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.627
So with Memorystore,
you don't have

00:09:49.627 --> 00:09:50.960
to worry about all those things.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.067
We basically take
care of things.

00:09:53.067 --> 00:09:54.650
In terms of how we
do health checking,

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:57.410
everything is done for you.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:01.660
And finally scalability
and security.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.480
With Memorystore, one of the
things again, one of our focus

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:06.620
is how can we make
things easier for you.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.730
So we have put in a lot of work
to make scaling super easy.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.400
Because some of the use cases
that we see with customers

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.750
is essentially spikes.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:17.080
For example, we have
a retail customer

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:22.740
where during Black Friday,
or any of their peak retail

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.270
or shopping days, it's
very unpredictable.

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:29.800
So it's great for us
to be able to be sure

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.460
that they can actually scale
up as needed without having

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:35.060
to go through a lot of pain.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.180
So that's kind of
provided in the service.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.790
The other key thing
is that Redis in a way

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:43.410
is not very secure
out of the box.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:48.280
So it's always a recommendation
that don't put Redis out there

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:49.940
in the public.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.280
So what we do is we deploy
Redis on a private IP.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.520
So it's not really
accessible from anywhere else

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:57.070
other than from within
the Google network.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:58.930
We use network-based
authentication

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:01.314
or network-based
security to limit access

00:11:01.314 --> 00:11:02.230
to the Redis instance.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.660
So there's an additional
level of security there.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:08.290
And also we provide
role-based access control

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:10.640
for the administrative
side of things.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:14.380
So bottom line is it's
a fully managed service

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:20.242
that allows you to just easily
deploy Redis and use it.

00:11:20.242 --> 00:11:21.950
And we have customers
currently using it.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:25.250
And it's seen a
pretty good adoption

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.710
in terms of a Memorystore.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.810
And it wasn't surprising,
because everybody

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:34.130
wants a cache, and Redis
is a very popular engine

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:34.970
that customers use.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.970
So we've been actually
getting pretty good responses

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:38.720
from customers.

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:43.760
So let's dive into a little
bit into what the offering

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:46.010
itself is and what we
have right now in beta.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:47.690
So one thing I
want to clarify is

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:49.231
that whatever I'm
talking about right

00:11:49.231 --> 00:11:52.370
now is what is available
today in the beta release.

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:54.980
And this is our v1 release.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:59.720
So the way we expose the
service is through two tiers.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:01.940
We have a basic tier
and a standard tier.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.340
So the basic tier is a
single Redis instance.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.700
And we see a lot of customers
just using the basic tier,

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:12.070
because in a lot of the use
cases it's a very simple cache.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.470
The key thing that
you want to kind of

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:18.270
be aware of in basic instance,
just like any other instance

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:18.770
of Redis--

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:22.640
single instance of Redis--
is that if this instance goes

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:24.170
down, it's a full cache flush.

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:28.910
For any reason behind the
scenes something happens,

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:30.770
we will make sure
that it comes back up,

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.389
but you will experience
a full cache flush.

00:12:33.389 --> 00:12:35.180
But that's some of the
advantages you have,

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:36.810
because now we do
the health check.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.720
And then we bring
back the instance,

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:40.572
we preserve the IP address.

00:12:40.572 --> 00:12:42.530
In some cases, when you
are deploying your own,

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:44.655
those are some of the
challenges that you run into,

00:12:44.655 --> 00:12:47.210
whether you can actually
reserve the same IP address when

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:48.960
an instance comes back up.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.190
So all of that is
kind of taken care of.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:54.710
We don't provide an
ancillary for basic tier.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:57.500
But in the standard tier, we
provide a replicated version

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:58.400
of Redis.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:01.520
We have across zone replication.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.130
And we provide
automatic failover.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:06.890
The other key thing is that you
connect to a single endpoint.

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:08.690
And we make sure
that the traffic

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:14.160
is directed to the right
instance, or right location.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.570
So in both the cases for
both basic and standard tier,

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.510
the key thing to remember is
that it's a single master.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:25.450
So even in the standard
tier, there's one master.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:28.190
It is not a scale-out
model-- yet.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:33.359
So that's something to keep in
mind when you use a service.

00:13:33.359 --> 00:13:35.400
And like I said, we will
be providing three nines

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.180
of availability once we go GA.

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.610
Right now we are in beta,
so the SLAs don't apply.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:42.490
But when we go GA,
we will provide

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:45.620
three nines of availability.

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:48.740
So provisioning.

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.200
One of the things
that we do is to try

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.560
to make things really simple.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.350
But we also
understand that using

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:56.090
UI is not the only
way folks want

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:57.590
to provision their services.

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.080
Nowadays with
infrastructure as a code

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:02.060
becoming more and
more of a standard way

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.700
to manage your
infrastructure, there

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:06.120
are different ways to do it.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.900
So with Cloud
Memorystore, we obviously

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:11.630
support the UI, SDK, et cetera.

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.640
But we also support
Cloud Deployment Manager,

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.860
or Deployment Manager
and also Terraform.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:19.400
So if you are using Terraform
to manage your infrastructure,

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:23.880
you can use that to
manage Memorystore.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:29.700
For the beta version, we
are supporting Redis 3.2.11.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:31.030
One of the things that I--

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:33.030
in talking about usefulness
of managed services,

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.590
one of the things that I
forgot to mention is recently,

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:38.940
I'm not sure if you're aware,
Redis had a recent security

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:43.320
vulnerability that was
announced, I think, last month.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.630
So the key thing is that we
actually applied the patch

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.300
even before the
vulnerability became public.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.100
So those are some of
the things that we

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:52.183
get to do behind the scenes
so that you don't have

00:14:52.183 --> 00:14:53.440
to worry about those things.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:57.510
So the 3.2.11 that we have
today is a Redis version

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:03.240
that is fully patched
with the latest fix.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:07.320
So going to the
next level, in terms

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.596
of how we expose the service,
what we allow you to do

00:15:10.596 --> 00:15:11.970
is we just allow
you to provision

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:16.920
any sites, from 1 GB to
300 GB, at 1 GB increment.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.630
But the way the
service is steered

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.580
is that the higher
the memory size,

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:22.710
you get better throughput.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:26.160
And also, there's M1 through
M5 tiering essentially

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.950
not only captures the
network performance,

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:33.510
but also relates to how
we price the service.

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:35.660
So in this particular
table, what you see

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.810
are the ranges that we provide,
and the corresponding network

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:39.820
throughput.

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:41.580
So if you need
higher throughput,

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.737
you basically have to
provision more memory

00:15:44.737 --> 00:15:45.570
based on your needs.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:51.120
But Redis being single threaded,
we provision enough CPUs

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:56.490
in the background that,
for certain workloads

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:00.280
our network may
become the bottleneck.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:04.710
So what we recommend is
that profile your workload,

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.526
run the benchmarks, and see
what works best for you.

00:16:07.526 --> 00:16:09.150
But the way we have
exposed the service

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:14.000
is essentially using
capacity tiers.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.020
And from a pricing
perspective, we

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:20.316
price the service
in gigabyte hours.

00:16:20.316 --> 00:16:21.690
For the different
ranges, we have

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:24.130
fixed gigabyte hour pricing.

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:27.820
And so when I say
gigabyte hour to somebody,

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:29.920
and it's like, what is
the pricing per hour?

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:31.080
So the pricing per
hour is essentially

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:32.550
a multiplier of
the gigabyte hour

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.485
price times the
amount of gigabyte

00:16:34.485 --> 00:16:36.020
you're provisioning
for that tier.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:38.040
So for example, if
you have provisioning

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:42.570
like 4 gigs of memory, it'll
be four times 4.9 cents,

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:48.535
or if you're provisioning 100
gigs, it's 100 times 1.9 cents.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.560
And we have a pricing
calculator that will easily

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:59.130
let you figure out what the
pricing is in terms of the per

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:00.270
hour pricing.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.940
But the key thing here is that
we give you the flexibility--

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.302
depending on what you
want from a cache--

00:17:05.302 --> 00:17:07.010
we give you the
flexibility, whether it's

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:09.210
a single instance, highly
available instance,

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:13.400
different throughput
characteristics, et cetera.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:16.460
So what I want to do
now is go to the demo

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:28.796
and walk you through the
provisioning process.

00:17:28.796 --> 00:17:30.170
And I just want
to dive into some

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:35.180
of the details of the managed
service, and some of the things

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.970
that you may want
to know about when

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:40.623
you're designing applications
on top of Memorystore.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.850
So Memorystore is
a storage product.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:53.340
And it can be found
under the Storage menu

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:56.310
in the Google Cloud Console.

00:17:56.310 --> 00:18:00.089
So what you see here
is a few instances

00:18:00.089 --> 00:18:01.130
that we have provisioned.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:03.810
So let me walk you through
creation of an instance.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:06.900
And I want to kind of talk
through some of the nuances

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:08.710
about the service.

00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:11.040
So I'm going to provision
an instance here.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:17.850
Let's say 4.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.760
The instance ID is essentially
the identifier of the instance.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:23.190
So like I mentioned earlier,
the version that we exposed

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.290
is 3.2.11.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.960
Immediately we get the question,
are you going to support 4.0?

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:29.740
The answer is yes.

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:31.889
We're going to add
4.0 pretty soon.

00:18:31.889 --> 00:18:33.930
But right now if you're
provisioning Memorystore,

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.540
you get 3.2.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.490
You get to choose a tier--

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:40.740
like I mentioned,
basic or standard tier.

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:45.060
And the locations that we
have currently exposed,

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:48.450
we have the services available
in five regions today.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:52.470
We are well aware of the fact
that there are more regions,

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:53.590
and we need to be there.

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:55.381
So that's something
that we are working on,

00:18:55.381 --> 00:18:59.110
getting more regions
added very quickly.

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:01.687
So what you see here when
you select the region,

00:19:01.687 --> 00:19:03.770
essentially, is Memorystore
is a regional service.

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:05.228
So what that means
is that when you

00:19:05.228 --> 00:19:08.910
deploy in a particular
region, applications

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.361
can connect from any zone.

00:19:11.361 --> 00:19:12.360
It's a regional service.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.640
So you can connect from
within that same region.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.150
We today don't support
cross region access.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:19.260
So if your application's
trying to connect

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:21.010
from one of the
region to Memorystore

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.770
in a different region,
we don't allow you to.

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:25.980
That is not enabled yet.

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:30.270
So we give you the option
to deploy your application

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:32.030
in any specific zone.

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:33.630
One thing I want to
note about pricing

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.990
is that we don't
charge for network.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.270
Memorystore doesn't
have network charge.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:42.426
But we do charge
for, for example,

00:19:42.426 --> 00:19:43.800
if your application
is in zone A,

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:45.900
and if Memorystore is
in zone B, and if you're

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:48.840
connecting from GCE, GCE has a
cross-net cross-zone networking

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:49.340
charge.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:52.830
So if that traffic
exists, then you

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:56.231
will be charged on the GCE
side for the cross-zone network

00:19:56.231 --> 00:19:56.730
traffic.

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:59.710
So that's something
to keep in mind.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:01.870
And we here, you basically
again, like I said,

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:04.860
you can provision
anywhere from 1 to 300 GB.

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:07.560
And depending on
what size you select,

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:11.130
you can see that the
network throughput changes.

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:14.520
And how much network
throughput you can get,

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:16.140
it changes with the size.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:18.720
So let's go ahead and pick
us a number of 30 gig.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:21.720
And finally, the network.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.990
So the way we authenticate
access to the Memorystore

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.450
is using VPC.

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:29.520
You pick a specific
network that you're

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:31.050
going to deploy Memorystore in.

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:33.660
And we restrict access
to Memorystore store

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:35.870
for that particular network.

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:39.210
So what that means is that
any application, or any VMs

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.809
on that specific
network can access

00:20:40.809 --> 00:20:41.850
this particular instance.

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:48.520
And finally, from a Redis
configuration perspective,

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.250
we haven't exposed too
many parameters yet.

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:52.600
We have only exposed two.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.450
Max memory policy
is one of them.

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:57.590
I believe [INAUDIBLE]
lru is our default.

00:20:57.590 --> 00:20:58.965
So this is something
that you may

00:20:58.965 --> 00:21:01.780
want to think about
configuring when you actually

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:06.040
deploy Memorystore based
on what you want to do.

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:07.090
And that's it.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:10.080
So once you do that, you
basically click Create.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.524
And you go ahead and create
the instance for you.

00:21:13.524 --> 00:21:15.940
So like I said, when you create
the instance, what you get

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:16.620
is an IP.

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:21.460
It's a private IP that
you get on the 6379 port.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:24.210
And I mentioned that Redis
is fully protocol compatible.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:26.560
So I can use redis-cli.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.480
Oh, I hope we can see it.

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:31.600
And they run commands
just like you

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:34.090
would do in any Redis instance.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:37.660
So it's an open source Redis.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.610
So all that is compatible.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:42.310
I would say, though, that from
a managed services perspective,

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:43.784
we do block some commands.

00:21:43.784 --> 00:21:45.700
So we have documented
what those commands are.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:49.630
But those commands are primarily
some of the admin commands

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:51.060
that we have blocked.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:54.310
From the application
side of things,

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:55.530
we support Lua scripting.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:56.590
We support Pub/Sub.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:01.680
So all that is fully available
when you use Memorystore.

00:22:06.154 --> 00:22:07.320
Let's go back to the slides.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:13.627
So from a best practice
perspective, like I said,

00:22:13.627 --> 00:22:14.460
you have the option.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:17.010
So if you're using
it as a simple cache,

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:19.470
basic tier will
definitely work for you.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:21.400
One thing I would note
is that with basic tier

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:22.858
we do have regular
maintenance that

00:22:22.858 --> 00:22:26.280
happens on a quarterly
basis, or whenever we have

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.775
to apply any critical patches.

00:22:28.775 --> 00:22:30.150
If you're using
a basic tier, you

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:33.250
can experience a full cache
flush in events like that.

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:35.730
With standard tier, what we
do is we do a rolling upgrade,

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.990
so you have higher availability
on events like that.

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:45.210
Because we just apply the
patch in a rolling manner.

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:47.710
The other key thing that I want
to point out is persistence.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:50.194
So in the beta release, we
haven't enabled persistence.

00:22:50.194 --> 00:22:52.110
When I say persistence,
I'm talking about AOF,

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:53.790
and I'm also talking about RDB.

00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:56.430
We haven't enabled
either one of them

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.700
yet on the basic
or standard tier.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:03.000
We are working on the ability
to import and export data

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:03.930
to a GCS bucket.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:05.840
That will be coming soon.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.220
We are also looking at
AOF persistence, which is

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:11.159
probably further down the road.

00:23:11.159 --> 00:23:13.200
From an instance sizing
perspective, like I said,

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:15.210
there are various benchmarks
that are currently available.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:16.834
I'm sure if you're
already using Redis,

00:23:16.834 --> 00:23:20.850
you have an idea of the
workload pattern that you have.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:23.940
Always run the benchmarks to
see what size fits for you.

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:27.179
Like I said, the higher the
size, the better the throughput

00:23:27.179 --> 00:23:27.720
that you get.

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:30.390
So you may want to do that.

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.640
Talked about the eviction
policy a little bit,

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.330
and also the configuration.

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:36.540
So we have documented all
the default configurations

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:37.170
that we use.

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:38.880
Like I said, we don't
allow you to change

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:42.060
a lot of the configurations,
or many of the configurations.

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.520
So it's good to know
what other configurations

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:48.701
that we set by default for
these Memorystore instances.

00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:56.450
How many of you actually do
scale their instances often?

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:56.950
Nobody.

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:02.920
So scaling is something that
we have enabled in beta.

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:05.330
It's a very simple
way to scale a system.

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:07.630
You basically change the size.

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:10.000
How does the behavior work?

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:14.980
In basic tier, it is a
full flush of the cache.

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:18.820
And in standard tier, we
basically do a rolling upgrade.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:22.030
Or when we do the
scaling, we do it

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:24.550
in a highly available manner.

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:26.680
So from an application
perspective, what that means

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:28.846
is that the only time the
application is unavailable

00:24:28.846 --> 00:24:33.100
if you're using standard tier
is when we do the failover.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:35.800
And the failover that we
do is actually very quick.

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:39.190
It's probably in
the 30-second range.

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:42.340
And we have some heuristics
in the back for replication.

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:45.370
But having said that,
talking about replication,

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.730
we use Redis replication
under the covers.

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:49.880
So that means it's an
asynchronous replication.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:53.230
So when you do
scaling or operation,

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:54.730
it's quite possible
that there could

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:57.040
be some unreplicated
data, or there

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:58.480
could be some data
that is stale,

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:02.100
because the changes
were not replicated

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.510
when the failover happened.

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:07.450
So that is instance scaling.

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:10.780
And I just wanted to
take a quick moment

00:25:10.780 --> 00:25:13.270
and see if folks have any
questions about the things

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:16.557
that I talked about till now.

00:25:16.557 --> 00:25:17.640
Do you mind using the mic?

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:28.280
AUDIENCE: So I think
one of the themes that's

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:30.446
been running through a lot
of the talks I've been to

00:25:30.446 --> 00:25:33.050
at this conference has been
don't trust the network, make

00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:34.430
sure you authenticate
your peers,

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:36.400
the whole service mesh thing.

00:25:36.400 --> 00:25:39.680
But this service seems
to, from what I can see,

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:41.900
be mostly have
VPC-level security.

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:45.620
Are there any plans to tie this
into IAM or TLS or something

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:48.759
so we can authenticate the
clients, in the managed server?

00:25:48.759 --> 00:25:49.550
GOPAL ASHOK: Right.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:54.000
So you're right that currently
there's VPC security.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:55.940
And one of the things
that we are working on

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.610
is initially at least
enable [? opp. ?]

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:01.521
But again, that's
just a Redis password.

00:26:01.521 --> 00:26:03.020
But beyond that,
yes, we are looking

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:07.310
at at least figuring out how
to do encrypted connections

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:08.610
between the client and server.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:11.272
So that's something
definitely on the road map.

00:26:11.272 --> 00:26:11.980
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:26:15.852 --> 00:26:21.130
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:22.130
GOPAL ASHOK: On-premise?

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:23.092
What do you mean?

00:26:23.092 --> 00:26:32.635
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:32.635 --> 00:26:34.760
GOPAL ASHOK: So are you
talking about connectivity,

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:36.530
or are you talking
about whether--

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:39.240
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the
cache [INAUDIBLE] applications.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:41.156
GOPAL ASHOK: So you want
to be able to connect

00:26:41.156 --> 00:26:43.670
to Memorystore from
on-prem using, let's say,

00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:47.460
a VPN, or something like that.

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.590
Can you tell me a
little bit more about--

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.270
because from a
caching perspective,

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:53.880
latency is a critical factor.

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:57.420
So I'm curious in that hybrid
case is more for migration,

00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:00.067
or what is the use case there?

00:27:00.067 --> 00:27:06.528
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] Some
of them are only on-premise.

00:27:06.528 --> 00:27:20.444
[INAUDIBLE]

00:27:20.444 --> 00:27:22.331
GOPAL ASHOK: Is it
Redis or Memcache?

00:27:22.331 --> 00:27:24.586
AUDIENCE: Right now, we
use the [INAUDIBLE] caching

00:27:24.586 --> 00:27:25.490
[INAUDIBLE].

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:26.467
GOPAL ASHOK: OK.

00:27:26.467 --> 00:27:27.800
So there's two questions there--

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:29.216
I think two parts
of the question.

00:27:29.216 --> 00:27:32.590
One is how do you connect from
on-prem onto Cloud Memorystore.

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:36.270
Again, that's something
that we haven't enabled yet.

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:39.190
We are trying to figure out,
see how many customers actually

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:39.690
need it.

00:27:39.690 --> 00:27:42.780
But we do see the
use case for it.

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:46.080
In terms of the terabyte size
cache, like I mentioned today,

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.590
we have the basic
and standard tier,

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:51.780
which is a single instance; and
the replicated instance, again,

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:52.590
single master.

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:54.690
You're right, it's 300 GB.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.792
From a terabyte
cache perspective,

00:27:56.792 --> 00:27:58.750
the next thing we are
working on is clustering.

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:01.890
So if you do scale-out
Redis clusters.

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:04.470
So when we have
scale-out Redis cluster,

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.050
you should be able to provide
terabytes of our provision

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:07.871
terabytes cache.

00:28:07.871 --> 00:28:09.620
So that's something
that we're working on.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:13.110
But you're right that currently
it is limited to 300 GP.

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:16.020
We may increase that
number a little bit more.

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:21.067
But we are still constrained
by that single VM deployment.

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.530
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
monitoring?

00:28:27.530 --> 00:28:30.167
GOPAL ASHOK: Yes that
was my next topic.

00:28:30.167 --> 00:28:31.250
One more question, please.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:32.420
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:35.420
So am I to understand
that because you're

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:39.005
using asynchronous replication
in a failover event in general,

00:28:39.005 --> 00:28:40.630
not just in a scaling,
that there could

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:42.210
be a small amount of data loss?

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:43.376
GOPAL ASHOK: That's correct.

00:28:43.376 --> 00:28:46.797
So yes, any time there is a
failover, you can have that.

00:28:46.797 --> 00:28:48.380
One of the things
that we are planning

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:50.570
to provide-- today we
don't provide a failover

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:52.830
API that you can
manually failover.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:54.440
So if you're doing
manual failovers,

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:55.898
once you have the
API, then you can

00:28:55.898 --> 00:29:00.320
control whether the instances
are in sync, and then failover.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:02.240
In that case, obviously
there won't be any.

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:08.000
But unplanned, yes, you can
expect unreplicated data.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:10.210
AUDIENCE: And
somewhat relatedly,

00:29:10.210 --> 00:29:14.090
how long would a failover
event be expected to last?

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:16.910
Here, when it's planned, it's
about 30 seconds, apparently.

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:19.400
But if it's unplanned,
about how long would

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:23.900
that period of strangeness and
possible unavailability be?

00:29:23.900 --> 00:29:26.170
GOPAL ASHOK: So
different scenarios.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:28.670
If it is a complete
failure, essentially we

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:31.899
expect it to be less than a
minute, if it's an unplanned,

00:29:31.899 --> 00:29:34.190
because essentially we are
now waiting for transactions

00:29:34.190 --> 00:29:37.460
to be replicated, or
commands to be replicated.

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:39.290
In the case of
scaling, we actually

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.470
have a heuristic where
we wait for the replica

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:43.970
to get caught up, and
then do the failover.

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:46.574
But then the failover time
is still less than a minute.

00:29:46.574 --> 00:29:47.240
AUDIENCE: Great.

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:47.740
Thanks.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.590
GOPAL ASHOK: There was a
question on monitoring.

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:53.720
So let me quickly go
back to the slides

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:57.650
and talk about
monitoring a little bit.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:29:59.240
So we do have
integrated monitoring.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:01.170
So what that means
is that we are

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:02.400
integrated with Stackdriver.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:05.240
So we export all our
metrics from the instances

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:06.950
into Stackdriver.

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:09.620
You can use Stackdriver
to basically do

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.200
the service-side monitoring.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:14.239
So let me quickly show
you what we have today

00:30:14.239 --> 00:30:15.530
from a Stackdriver perspective.

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:16.446
Switching to the demo.

00:30:18.825 --> 00:30:21.200
So if you go into Stackdriver--
it's a little bit zoomed.

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:23.560
I'm going to zoom
in a little bit.

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:35.210
So if you go into Metrics
Explorer in Stackdriver,

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:43.000
and if you look for Memorystore,
see your Cloud Memorystore

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:43.840
Redis instance.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:45.340
And underneath that,
we have exposed

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:47.890
a whole bunch of
metrics that you can

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:51.050
use to monitor your instances.

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:53.582
So what I've done
is we haven't yet

00:30:53.582 --> 00:30:54.790
have an integrated dashboard.

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:56.998
But you can go ahead and
create a dashboard yourself.

00:30:56.998 --> 00:31:00.820
And this is a dashboard
that I've created.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:04.870
So as you can see, you basically
can build your own dashboards,

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:10.600
and monitor the Cloud
Memorystore instance

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:11.380
from Stackdriver.

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:13.441
So we have that
integrated monitoring.

00:31:13.441 --> 00:31:15.940
And the other thing is that if
you are using your own tools,

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:17.648
you should be able to
get the metrics out

00:31:17.648 --> 00:31:20.119
of Memorystore, because we
are fully protocol compliant.

00:31:20.119 --> 00:31:22.660
One thing you may want to test
is if there is any other block

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:25.630
commands that we are using,
blocks any of the tools,

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:28.450
we would be happy to kind
of take a look at that,

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:30.650
and see if we need to
do something there.

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:33.370
But we haven't had
any yet in terms

00:31:33.370 --> 00:31:37.750
of those commands blocking the
usage of third-party tools.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:39.970
But like I said, with
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:43.040
you get all the metrics
from the Redis side,

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:45.527
like network throughput
CPU utilization.

00:31:45.527 --> 00:31:47.610
One of the interesting
things that you can do also

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:52.630
is you can monitor the create
alerts for specific metrics,

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:54.520
and basically alert you.

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:57.470
One of the good examples
is like memory usage ratio.

00:31:57.470 --> 00:32:00.910
So if you want to be able to be
sure that your instance is not

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:04.150
running out of memory, you
can actually set an alert.

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:07.660
And Karthi's actually going
to demo this in a section,

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:08.680
I believe.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:14.560
And also do
alert-based monitoring.

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:18.940
So going back to the slides,
the service-side monitoring

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:19.780
is interesting.

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:21.890
So it gives you a bunch of data.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:23.710
But it still really doesn't--

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:25.686
can we go back to
the slide, please?

00:32:25.686 --> 00:32:28.300
Oh, we're back on the slide.

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:31.551
So it still doesn't give
you end-to-end visibility,

00:32:31.551 --> 00:32:33.550
in terms of what's happening
from an application

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:34.720
perspective.

00:32:34.720 --> 00:32:36.220
A lot of the times
you struggle when

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:38.345
you have a latency problem
on the application side.

00:32:38.345 --> 00:32:40.749
You come in, look at
the server-side metrics.

00:32:40.749 --> 00:32:42.540
But you are still not
sure what's going on.

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:43.900
Unless there is
something obvious that's

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:45.524
going on on the server
side, it becomes

00:32:45.524 --> 00:32:48.050
extremely hard to troubleshoot.

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:49.787
So the interesting thing--

00:32:49.787 --> 00:32:52.120
and one of the things that
Google has been trying to do,

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:56.470
and Google has actually
open sourced is OpenCensus.

00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:59.340
How many of you use
OpenCensus today?

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:03.730
So OpenCensus is
essentially a distribution

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:05.650
of libraries that
basically allows

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:08.800
you to collect traces and
metrics from your application.

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:12.430
It's a super powerful
framework, or a set of libraries

00:33:12.430 --> 00:33:14.500
that we have open sourced.

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:17.740
And one of the things that
we've been doing internally

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:20.370
is to base instruments,
some of the Redis clients

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:23.450
that are out there, and
see how it all works.

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:25.390
But at a high level,
what OpenCensus does

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:28.780
is it basically allows
you to use the libraries

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:31.439
and inject your own or
export your own metrics

00:33:31.439 --> 00:33:32.855
from your application
perspective.

00:33:32.855 --> 00:33:34.810
So depending on what
your application does,

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:36.130
you can define what matters.

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:36.940
Is it latency?

00:33:36.940 --> 00:33:38.265
Is it transactions per second?

00:33:38.265 --> 00:33:38.890
Whatever it is.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:42.430
But at the application level.

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:46.990
And then what you can do is
the OpenCensus exports it

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:50.680
into some of the popular
monitoring and tracing tools,

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:52.900
one of them being Stackdriver.

00:33:52.900 --> 00:33:54.485
So now you have
end-to-end visibility

00:33:54.485 --> 00:33:56.110
not just from the
server side, but also

00:33:56.110 --> 00:33:57.910
from the application side.

00:33:57.910 --> 00:34:01.660
So this makes troubleshooting
extremely, extremely much more

00:34:01.660 --> 00:34:03.970
easier, and provides
end-to-end visibility

00:34:03.970 --> 00:34:07.340
compared to just having
server-side metric.

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:09.460
So having said that, what
we want to do right now

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:12.257
is to show you how
you can actually bring

00:34:12.257 --> 00:34:13.340
all these things together.

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:16.480
So Karthi is going
to kind of walk you

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:19.360
through how you can build
applications, build,

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:22.150
deploy, and actually monitor
application also using

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:22.870
OpenCensus.

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:28.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:29.960
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN:
Thanks, Gopal.

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:32.070
Can everyone hear me?

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:33.290
Great.

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:37.429
So let me actually
move to the next slide.

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:41.211
As Gopal mentioned,
the engineering team

00:34:41.211 --> 00:34:42.710
responsible for
Memorystore, they've

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:47.630
done a great job surfacing all
the metrics that you just saw.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:50.850
You guys are able to
see latency, errors,

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:53.040
things of that nature
on the server side.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:55.670
So what we're going
to see next is

00:34:55.670 --> 00:34:59.990
how can we instrument our own
application with an emphasis

00:34:59.990 --> 00:35:02.820
on using Memorystore.

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:05.120
So I'll talk about
a simple application

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:09.770
architecture that's a three-tier
application that uses Java.

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:12.690
And we're also going to use
a traditional database--

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:15.460
in this case MySQL
on top of Cloud SQL.

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:20.090
And we'll also get into
deployment and provisioning.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:24.200
So I have a quick, informal
survey here for provisioning.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:28.830
We're going to be using
Terraform and Kubernetes

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:30.020
as the topology.

00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:32.870
So how many of you are using
Terraform and Kubernetes

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:34.261
across your applications?

00:35:34.261 --> 00:35:34.760
Great.

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:36.860
So it'll be relevant to
a good number of people.

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:38.240
Happy to see that.

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:42.830
This is the simple
three-tier application

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:44.950
that I talked about.

00:35:44.950 --> 00:35:49.850
As you can see, we
have, IN this case,

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:52.840
it's a single-page
application that uses Vue.js.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:56.890
It's going to talk through
our global HTTP load balancer

00:35:56.890 --> 00:36:02.782
into a Java Spring API
hosted on top of a GKE.

00:36:02.782 --> 00:36:04.240
That application
is, in turn, going

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:09.760
to talk to Cloud SQL,
MySQL in this case.

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:12.239
And essentially
this application,

00:36:12.239 --> 00:36:13.780
which I'll demo in
a little bit, will

00:36:13.780 --> 00:36:15.850
look up employees
in our organization,

00:36:15.850 --> 00:36:20.320
and cache those results
into Memorystore for Redis.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:24.107
And the hope is you'll
get really low latency

00:36:24.107 --> 00:36:25.690
after it's been
cached, because you're

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:27.340
using an in-memory cache.

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:32.840
And a little bit about
infrastructure as code.

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:37.344
As Gopal mentioned, we'll be
using infrastructure as code.

00:36:37.344 --> 00:36:39.010
I mean, this is all
the rage these days.

00:36:39.010 --> 00:36:41.890
It makes DevOps a
whole lot easier.

00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:44.680
We can do cool things like
configuration management,

00:36:44.680 --> 00:36:47.440
keep track of what our resources
are, all that good stuff.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:52.730
Is everybody familiar--
or are most people

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:56.110
familiar-- with VPC,
and the whole notion

00:36:56.110 --> 00:36:58.760
of networking on GCP?

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:00.880
So assuming that,
what we're going to do

00:37:00.880 --> 00:37:04.600
is we're going to deploy a
GKE cluster into our VPC.

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:09.370
This is a separate VPC set
aside for our application.

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:12.310
And into the same VPC, we're
going to deploy a Memorystore

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:13.430
instance.

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:14.950
This instance, as
Gopal mentioned,

00:37:14.950 --> 00:37:20.170
has to be in the same VPC
so that we isolate traffic

00:37:20.170 --> 00:37:23.440
to that instance from
any other resources that

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:26.330
may be trying to access it.

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:29.230
We also have a whole bunch
of other GCP resources

00:37:29.230 --> 00:37:30.330
that we'll be using.

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:33.580
And I'll get into
it in a little bit.

00:37:33.580 --> 00:37:38.020
On the Terraform side,
this is something

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:41.800
that our product teams
have been really good at.

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:44.020
Every time we
release a product, we

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:48.250
ensure that there's deployment
manager support, as well

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:49.720
as Terraform support.

00:37:49.720 --> 00:37:51.550
In this case, it's
fairly straightforward

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:56.690
to declaratively define
our Redis instance,

00:37:56.690 --> 00:37:58.390
or Memorystore instance.

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:01.240
The two things that I'll call
out here are, as you can see,

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:04.060
there's an authorized
network specified there.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:06.520
That network is the
same as the GKE cluster

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:10.174
that I'm going to show
you in a little bit.

00:38:10.174 --> 00:38:11.590
The other thing
that I'll call out

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:14.000
is the fact that there's
a reserved IP range.

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.180
This keeps things clean.

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:17.800
We're documenting
the IP range we're

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:20.080
going to be using for
our Redis instance,

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:23.360
as well as our pods
in the GKE cluster.

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:28.660
And here's our GKE cluster.

00:38:28.660 --> 00:38:30.040
The only thing
I'll call out here

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:35.050
is the fact that I'm specifying
an IP allocation policy.

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:39.400
And the reason for doing that
is because we're using GKE,

00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:42.400
we have to enable IP aliasing
in order for our pods

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:43.960
to be able to talk
to Memorystore.

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:45.418
This is an extra
step that you have

00:38:45.418 --> 00:38:49.630
to do, as opposed to if you
were deploying a simple VM,

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:53.560
and having that talk to
your Memorystore instance,

00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:54.710
not much else to do there.

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:57.930
But in this case, you have
to enable IP aliasing.

00:38:57.930 --> 00:38:59.977
The steps for this might
look a little different

00:38:59.977 --> 00:39:02.310
if you're provisioning these
resources using, let's say,

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:05.189
G-Cloud, or Deployment Manager.

00:39:05.189 --> 00:39:06.980
And I also want to call
out the fact that--

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:09.190
I'm going to go back
a couple of slides--

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:11.050
all the resources
that you see here

00:39:11.050 --> 00:39:15.100
can be deployed with Google
Deployment Manager as well.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:16.580
I'm choosing to
use Terraform here.

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:21.740
With that said, let's
get into the demo.

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:23.390
Thankfully my machine
has not locked.

00:39:28.540 --> 00:39:29.710
Awesome.

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:32.260
So actually, the first
step that I'm going to do

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:35.620
is I'm going to
show you that I can

00:39:35.620 --> 00:39:38.620
have a pod on my
GKE cluster talk

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:42.070
to the Memorystore instance.

00:39:42.070 --> 00:39:43.540
As Gopal showed you earlier--

00:39:43.540 --> 00:39:45.730
I'm just going to refresh
your memory on this--

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:47.620
we have three instances.

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:50.250
These are all for the
purposes of our demo.

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:52.360
And they're all deployed
into different VPCs.

00:39:52.360 --> 00:39:54.970
And even if you see some
of those instances having

00:39:54.970 --> 00:39:57.700
the same IP address,
don't get confused.

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:59.320
They're all in different VPCs.

00:39:59.320 --> 00:40:02.240
That's why they have
the same IP address.

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:06.000
So we know that we want to
be able to ping from our pod

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:08.141
10.0.0.4.

00:40:08.141 --> 00:40:09.640
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:18.210
to connect to the junpod, where
I have Redis CLI deployed.

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:20.020
It happens to be
called mysqlclient,

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:22.830
because that's also where
the MySQL client is.

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:25.830
And I connect to it.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:28.780
And let's spin up Redis CLI.

00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:34.110
That's the IP address,
as I mentioned.

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:36.480
And I can list the
characteristics

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:37.690
of that instance.

00:40:37.690 --> 00:40:38.730
So all good.

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:41.350
And this is what you would do
in your application as well.

00:40:41.350 --> 00:40:43.770
And once there, let
me flush the cache

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:47.320
so that I can get the
demo to work as expected.

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:47.820
Cool.

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:49.903
So now let me go to the
demo and kind of show you.

00:40:49.903 --> 00:40:51.960
It's pretty simple.

00:40:51.960 --> 00:40:54.370
It's an application that,
once again, as I said,

00:40:54.370 --> 00:40:57.790
it allows you to look up the
employees in your organization.

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:00.570
I am going to start
typing in Gopal.

00:41:00.570 --> 00:41:03.180
And there's one step
that I have to do.

00:41:03.180 --> 00:41:06.300
We have something
called BeyondCorp

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:09.420
that interferes with my demo.

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:10.980
I'm going to turn that off.

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:13.830
And now let me type in Gopal.

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:17.160
And what this demo has is,
in addition to all the names

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:19.440
that it brings back,
it also shows you

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:21.732
how long that request took.

00:41:21.732 --> 00:41:23.190
Hopefully, all of
you can see this.

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:27.100
In this case it took
539 milliseconds.

00:41:27.100 --> 00:41:28.980
Now the hope is
it's been cached.

00:41:28.980 --> 00:41:32.490
I type in G-O-P again.

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:38.340
And let me refresh this
just for good measure.

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:40.597
It took much less time.

00:41:40.597 --> 00:41:42.180
And I know that's
not very convincing.

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:44.230
What we're going to do
is, as Gopal mentioned,

00:41:44.230 --> 00:41:46.500
we're going to use
OpenCensus, which

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:47.910
I'll show you the code for.

00:41:47.910 --> 00:41:49.410
We're going to use
OpenCensus to see

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:57.340
what happened during that call
path as that call progressed.

00:41:57.340 --> 00:42:02.610
For this, I will switch to
my Google Cloud console.

00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:08.780
Let me pull up the console here.

00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:12.810
So just so you guys can go
to this console on your own,

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:15.830
this is under the
Stackdriver Trace menu.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:19.430
And I'm going to look
at the trace list.

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:23.280
And let me make this
a little bit smaller,

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:26.170
because it's hard to see.

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:29.600
This was my initial call.

00:42:29.600 --> 00:42:33.500
I typed in G-O-P for Gopal.

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:38.980
And you can see that
this call started

00:42:38.980 --> 00:42:40.580
at the getEmployees method.

00:42:40.580 --> 00:42:44.510
And it actually shows you
how long that method took--

00:42:44.510 --> 00:42:46.604
538 milliseconds or so.

00:42:46.604 --> 00:42:48.020
But the cool thing
here is you can

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:52.580
tell that this call actually
looked up, or tried to look up

00:42:52.580 --> 00:42:56.360
that keyword in Redis.

00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:58.010
We're using the
Jedis library here,

00:42:58.010 --> 00:42:59.509
and I'll talk a
little bit about how

00:42:59.509 --> 00:43:01.460
that library's instrumented.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:03.590
But it didn't find that keyword.

00:43:03.590 --> 00:43:05.750
So then it had to go to MySQL.

00:43:05.750 --> 00:43:08.585
And it also shows you how
long that MySQL call took.

00:43:08.585 --> 00:43:13.340
And you can tell the bulk of
the time was in the MySQL call.

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:16.150
Execute query, et cetera.

00:43:16.150 --> 00:43:18.610
It found the entry.

00:43:18.610 --> 00:43:22.240
And then it put the results of
that request into our cache.

00:43:22.240 --> 00:43:24.940
So something straightforward
that most people do

00:43:24.940 --> 00:43:27.689
in a caching type environment.

00:43:27.689 --> 00:43:29.230
So you can kind of
see what happened.

00:43:29.230 --> 00:43:30.188
This is the happy path.

00:43:30.188 --> 00:43:32.030
This is what you
expect to happen.

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:34.150
So now let's go back
to our trace list,

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:39.190
and just confirm that
once it's been cached,

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:40.450
I type in the same key word.

00:43:40.450 --> 00:43:44.954
In this case, hey, it
pulled it out of the cache.

00:43:44.954 --> 00:43:46.120
And it took a lot less time.

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:48.940
And you can see that it
didn't have to go to MySQL.

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:50.120
So that's the happy path.

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:55.550
Oh, before I go back
and introduce an error,

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:58.000
let me also show you,
in addition to tracing,

00:43:58.000 --> 00:43:59.470
the cool thing that
OpenCensus does

00:43:59.470 --> 00:44:02.920
is it also lets you
surface metrics.

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:04.670
I had a whole bunch
of metrics shown here.

00:44:04.670 --> 00:44:07.300
But unfortunately a whole
bunch of time has elapsed.

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:11.170
So this is what happened
for my most recent query.

00:44:11.170 --> 00:44:13.240
You can see things
like heat map.

00:44:13.240 --> 00:44:16.390
So you get a sense of
what your queries are

00:44:16.390 --> 00:44:18.280
doing in an aggregate.

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:22.150
You can also see a linear view
of what your queries are doing.

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:24.370
In addition to that,
you can surface

00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:28.660
errors from whatever Redis
library you're using.

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:31.450
In this case I've
instrumented Jedis.

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:34.610
And I'll show you how to
surface those errors as well.

00:44:34.610 --> 00:44:37.360
Before I do that, let me
show you the code real quick.

00:44:40.477 --> 00:44:41.560
Can everybody see this OK?

00:44:41.560 --> 00:44:42.760
Is it too small?

00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:44.340
Hopefully, it's big enough.

00:44:44.340 --> 00:44:46.560
This is the getEmployees
call that I mentioned.

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:50.520
It's my Spring Boot controller.

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:54.150
And I'm importing the
appropriate OpenCensus

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:55.410
libraries.

00:44:55.410 --> 00:44:59.220
Gopal showed you where
to get that library.

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:05.190
And I'm using something
called the tracer class.

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:06.780
And I set up a span.

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:10.050
And in that span I
can put in annotations

00:45:10.050 --> 00:45:13.115
that show me which
particular method was called,

00:45:13.115 --> 00:45:15.490
any data that was passed into
that method-- in this case,

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:18.590
the keyword that was supplied.

00:45:18.590 --> 00:45:23.550
And I can trace methods that are
internal to that call as well.

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:26.780
In this case, I'm also
instrumented getFromCache.

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:29.720
I've added an annotation
that says cache miss, in case

00:45:29.720 --> 00:45:30.980
there is a cache miss.

00:45:30.980 --> 00:45:32.990
Let me show you that real quick.

00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:33.926
Go back to this.

00:45:38.030 --> 00:45:41.190
And you can see that annotation.

00:45:41.190 --> 00:45:43.380
So it shows up in my
Stackdriver console.

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:48.670
So it's pretty straightforward
to annotate your code

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:49.422
with OpenCensus.

00:45:49.422 --> 00:45:51.130
The other thing I'll
call out is the fact

00:45:51.130 --> 00:45:54.940
that you're seeing, going back
to this real quick, you're

00:45:54.940 --> 00:45:57.970
seeing all this stuff
from the Jedis library,

00:45:57.970 --> 00:46:02.110
like jedis.protocol.bulk,
process bulk reply,

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:04.510
redis.clients.jedis.close.

00:46:04.510 --> 00:46:05.240
All that stuff.

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:06.970
I didn't instrument
that in my code.

00:46:06.970 --> 00:46:09.310
That came from the
instrumentation

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:11.740
that is in the library
that I'm using.

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:14.204
So out of the box,
obviously the Jedis library

00:46:14.204 --> 00:46:16.120
is not going to give you
this instrumentation.

00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:18.495
And I'll show you where to
get that instrumented library.

00:46:18.495 --> 00:46:20.430
But for your own
needs, if you're

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:24.490
using a version of Redis
library for whatever language

00:46:24.490 --> 00:46:26.890
you're using, look
for it in this place.

00:46:26.890 --> 00:46:29.410
If it's not there, you can
obviously fairly easily

00:46:29.410 --> 00:46:33.074
instrument it and contribute
it back to the community.

00:46:33.074 --> 00:46:34.750
Let me see how
much time we have.

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:37.600
We have a little bit more
time to show the error

00:46:37.600 --> 00:46:41.570
condition that I described.

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:45.235
So how do you surface
errors that you may see.

00:46:45.235 --> 00:46:46.610
So for that, what
I'm going to do

00:46:46.610 --> 00:46:52.340
is I'm going to trigger
this thing that essentially

00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:54.810
has my application talk to
a non-existent Redis host.

00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:57.600
So now I'm going
to type in Gopal.

00:46:57.600 --> 00:46:59.240
Even though Gopal
was cached, you

00:46:59.240 --> 00:47:03.950
can see that it's still
taking a lot more time.

00:47:03.950 --> 00:47:08.540
Let me switch back
to my trace window

00:47:08.540 --> 00:47:10.060
here, and see what happened.

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:15.750
I'm going to quickly refresh
this Stackdriver console.

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:20.432
So this is my trace
from just now.

00:47:20.432 --> 00:47:24.580
And click on the trace
and see what happened.

00:47:24.580 --> 00:47:28.330
You can see that it had
attempted to connect to Redis.

00:47:28.330 --> 00:47:30.220
And it failed.

00:47:30.220 --> 00:47:32.710
And it went to MySQL.

00:47:32.710 --> 00:47:34.630
So something
happened that caused

00:47:34.630 --> 00:47:37.090
it to close the connection.

00:47:37.090 --> 00:47:40.810
And I can also see in
my Stackdriver console

00:47:40.810 --> 00:47:44.140
that errors are
starting to show up.

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:51.840
And I can trigger alerts
based on this error as well.

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:54.640
What I've done
here is I've set up

00:47:54.640 --> 00:48:01.870
an alert that says if there
is more than one error

00:48:01.870 --> 00:48:04.180
for this particular
metric that I'm surfacing,

00:48:04.180 --> 00:48:05.617
send me an email.

00:48:05.617 --> 00:48:07.450
You can also have a
connect to page or duty,

00:48:07.450 --> 00:48:11.365
or a whole slew of other
integration points.

00:48:14.110 --> 00:48:15.712
So hopefully that
gives you a sense

00:48:15.712 --> 00:48:17.170
